{"ts": 1789778185.8202748, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "{13, 17, \u221227} at 1.163 remains the clear winner \u2014 no substitution comes within 0.1 of it. It's a robust local optimum, chosen from a well-structured neighborhood rather than a lucky outlier.\n\n**Selection shrinkage considerations:** I searched roughly 60 specifications. The in-sample 1.163 is the max over that set, so it carries upward selection bias. With n=5000 daily observations (~20 years), the standard error of a Sharpe estimate is about \u221a(1/5000)\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 annualized \u2014 meaningful relative to a 1.16 estimate. Two factors temper my discount, though:\n\n- The winner beats the *second*-best family (17\u221227 pairs, ~1.03\u20131.07) by a modest, plausible margin rather than an implausible leap, which is what genuine signal looks like.\n- All three features are individually strong (0.72, 0.69, 0.63) with consistent signs, and every constituent pair is strong (0.93\u20130.98). The trio isn't held together by one fragile interaction.\n\nAgainst that, the three features are clearly correlated: the trio's 1.163 is far below the ~1.2 (\u221a3 \u00d7 0.68) that independent signals would give, so the marginal gain from the third feature is real but partly overlapping. Some of the pairwise-to-triple lift will not replicate.\n\nMy central estimate discounts to roughly **0.95** \u2014 shaving about 0.2 for selection bias and correlated-signal decay. I'll use a standard deviation of 0.25 to reflect both estimation error and the risk that the feature relationships shift out of sample."}
