{"ts": 1789778226.001759, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long feature 13, long feature 17, short feature 27 \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.163**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.95 \u00b1 0.25\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Univariate sweep** of all 40 features. Strongest: 17 (+0.72), 13 (+0.69), 27 (\u22120.63), 7 (\u22120.46), 12 (\u22120.45), 32 (+0.43).\n2. **Pairwise search** over the top candidates, orienting each feature by its univariate sign. Best pairs: 17+13 (0.98), 17\u221227 (0.96), 13\u221227 (0.93).\n3. **Triple search**, extending the best pairs with every strong candidate plus a set of weak-univariate features as diversification tests. {13, 17, \u221227} won at 1.163, ahead of the next best (1.067).\n4. **Sign-flip checks** confirmed orientation \u2014 flipping either 13 or 27 collapsed the Sharpe to 0.392, confirming the signs are driven by real structure and not arbitrary.\n\n### On the prediction\n\nI deliberately predicted well below the in-sample figure. The 1.163 is the maximum over ~60 evaluated specs, so it is biased upward by selection, and with n=5000 the standard error on any single Sharpe is ~0.22 annualized.\n\nThe main substantive caveat is that these three features are **correlated, not independent**. Three independent signals of ~0.68 would have combined to roughly \u221a3 \u00d7 0.68 \u2248 1.2; the observed 1.163 falls short, indicating meaningful overlap. The third feature adds genuine but partly redundant information, and some of that incremental lift will not replicate.\n\nWhat supports the choice: all three features are individually strong with consistent signs, all three constituent pairs are strong (0.93\u20130.98), and the winner's margin over the runner-up family is modest and plausible rather than an implausible jump. The spec doesn't depend on one fragile interaction \u2014 the signal is distributed across the combination, which is the profile that tends to survive out of sample.\n\nNetting these, I shaved ~0.2 off the in-sample value and used sd = 0.25 to cover both estimation error and the risk of relationships shifting out of sample."}
